				\begin{document}$${\text{Biodegradation}}\;{\text{Ratio}} = \frac{{{\text{(Initial}}\;{\text{weight}}\;{\text{of}}\;{\text{sample}} - {\text{Weight}}\;{\text{of}}\;{\text{sample}}\;{\text{in}}\;{\text{time}}\;{\text{T)}}}}{{{\text{Initial}}\;{\text{weight}}\;{\text{of}}\;{\text{sample}}}}$$\end{document} Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) study The thermal stability of the polymer raw materials and nanocomposite scaffolds was assessed using TGA. A thermogravimetric analyzer (TGA-PL, TGA 1500, Canada) was employed for this analysis, conducted in a nitrogen atmosphere within the 25–600°C temperature range. Investigation of scaffold biomineralization using simulated body fluid (SBF) The performance of the scaffold bone graft was assessed using simulated body fluid (SBF), which was prepared following established protocols with chemical reagents. SBF is essential for examining the formation of biomaterials on polymer scaffolds. To prepare the solution, 1000 mL of SBF was made by adding 700 mL of deionized water into a plastic container in a water bath. It is recommended to cover the container with either a glass or plastic lid while stirring; for the process to be successful, the solution should be kept at a consistent temperature of 36.5 °C throughout. Adding the ingredients in order is essential; once each ingredient is dissolved completely, the next one should be added, and so on. As soon as the ingredients were added, hydrochloric acid was used in the amount of 0–5 ml with an additive solution to achieve a pH of 7.4 in the resulting solution after adding the ingredients. A total of 1000 mL of solution was prepared by adding water to a flask and shaking it at 20 °C to reach the desired volume. The solution was then transferred to a polyethylene container and stored in a refrigerator at 5–10 °C. Scaffold samples were immersed in the SBF solution and incubated at 37 °C for 14 days. After incubation, the samples were removed, rinsed with distilled water, and dried using the freeze-drying method. Finally, EDX and SEM techniques were employed to evaluate the biomineralization capability of the scaffold samples 38 . Assessment of toxicity and cell viability First, uniform pieces of scaffolds were cut and immersed in 70% ethanol solution for 30 min to sterilize them. Eventually, we must continue using PBS to remove any remaining ethanol from the system and transfer it to a 96-well cell culture plate. Then, mesenchymal stem cells were cultured on the scaffolds with a cell density of 1 × 10 4  cells per cell plate. After collecting the samples, they were cultured inside the incubator in a DMEM medium containing 1% penicillin–streptomycin and 10% FBS. They were incubated for 72 h at 37 °C. After 72 h, the supernatant of all the wells of the 96-well plate was removed, and after washing with PBS to each well, 0.2 ml of MTT solution (5 mg/mm) was added, and the wells were kept in the incubator for four hours. It was incubated at 37 °C. A solution of 0.1 mL DMSO was then substituted for the MTT solution for 30 min. Finally, the absorbance was measured by an ELISA reader at a wavelength of 570 nm to determine cell viability.

